    "body": "**TLDR:** Verifiers env-server workers no longer load torch, and they give a finished episode's memory back to the OS. Each worker, after it builds the Qwen3.5 renderer, goes from 776 to 341 MB of anonymous memory (RSS 1,163 -> 384 MB): ~7 GB per host for the Terminal-Bench recipes' 16 workers (estimate: 16 x 435 MB).\n\n**Problem**\n1. The env server runs its pool workers on the controller host: 16 in the Terminal-Bench recipes. An elastic pool adds a worker for every `multiplex` rollouts in flight.\n2. **Torch.** Each worker builds a renderer, whose tokenizer loads through `transformers.AutoTokenizer`. In transformers 5.19 that import pulls in torch: ~435 MB of anonymous memory per worker, never used.\n3. **Peak RSS.** Workers render on threads, glibc gives threads their own malloc pools (arenas), and by default it keeps the memory those threads free.\n4. **Cost:** 16 x 435 MB = ~7 GB per host from torch alone (estimate). A pool with ~4,400 rollouts in flight at `multiplex=8` would have ~600 workers, ~260 GB (estimate).\n\n**Solution**\n1. The worker hook `setup_env_server_worker` (torch-free `worker_setup.py`) now sets `sys.modules[\"torch\"] = None` first. Verifiers runs this hook in every worker before it builds the renderer.\n2. `import torch` then raises ImportError, and transformers loads the tokenizer without it. The env-server process already imported torch through torchtitan, so the hook leaves torch alone there.\n3. Before it spawns workers, `_run_env_server_process` sets `MALLOC_ARENA_MAX=2`, `MALLOC_MMAP_THRESHOLD_=65536` (every block of 64 KB or more gets its own mmap and goes back to the OS when freed) and `MALLOC_TRIM_THRESHOLD_=0` (free memory at the top of a heap goes back at once). glibc reads them when a worker starts.\n4. The arena cap is assigned, not defaulted: the env-server process inherits the controller host's own cap (16 once the per-role arena caps land), and the workers need 2. A launcher's two thresholds still win.\n5. Unchanged: the controller, trainer and generators, and the env-server process itself.\n\n**Results**\n```text\nOne worker after building the Qwen3.5 renderer and rendering a prompt.\nCPU, real VerifiersEnvServer with 2 pool workers, Qwen/Qwen3.5-27B tokenizer, read from /proc/<pid>.\n                       main        this PR\nRssAnon (anonymous)    776 MB  ->  341 MB\nVmRSS                1,163 MB  ->  384 MB\nlibtorch_cpu mapped      yes   ->  no\n```\n\n```text\nThe glibc variables. Churn test: 8 threads, 8 episodes of 62k tokens alive at a time, 48 in total.\n3-4 runs each, RSS above the 166 MB baseline.\n                              glibc default        2 arenas + 64 KB threshold\npeak                          +475..506 MB    ->   +184..226 MB\nafter every episode is freed  +147..306 MB    ->    +33..36 MB\nwall time (CPU-bound)         9.6 s           ->   11.4-12.3 s\n```\nThe extra CPU time: blocks of 64 KB or more become mmap/munmap pairs. Workers mostly wait on the model and the sandboxes.\n\nYou may ask:\n- Why not `USE_TORCH=0`? transformers 5.19 still imports torch with it.\n- Why in the `log_setup` hook? It is the only hook Verifiers runs in every worker before the renderer exists. Workers are fresh `spawn` interpreters, so setting `sys.modules` in the env-server process doesn't reach them.\n- Why not `malloc_trim(0)` after each episode? The variables match it with no code: after the episodes end, +23..25 MB with a trim per episode vs +23..33 MB with the variables (single thread, 3 runs).\n- Why `MALLOC_TRIM_THRESHOLD_` too? The churn numbers came out the same without it, but it costs one line and matches the settings of the long Terminal-Bench runs.\n\nNOTE:\n- A worker now fails with ImportError if it imports torch. `datasets.Dataset.map` and `IterableDataset` iteration fail too: they import torch whenever `\"torch\" in sys.modules`, which the block makes true. Workers build tasks from the request and never load data; the Terminal-Bench taskset is torch-free (tested).\n- The DAPO-Math taskset imports torch, so its recipe must keep `num_workers=1`, where the env-server process serves rollouts itself and keeps torch.\n- Not run: a GPU RL run. The numbers above were measured before the rebase onto the taskset-alias fix.\n\n**Test plan**\n```bash\npytest tests/rl/unit_tests/cpu/test_verifiers.py tests/rl/unit_tests/cpu/test_verifiers_terminal_bench.py \\\n  tests/rl/unit_tests/cpu/test_verifiers_example.py\n# test_verifiers_terminal_bench.py + test_verifiers_example.py: 14 passed on Verifiers 0.3.1 and on 0.4.0\npytest tests/rl/unit_tests/cpu   # 532 passed, 3 skipped (Verifiers 0.3.1)\n```\n- `test_env_server_workers_skip_the_trainer_imports` imports the worker's spawn target, runs the pickled `log_setup` in a fresh interpreter, resolves the taskset by its alias, then imports `transformers.AutoTokenizer`: torch must stay unloaded. Without the block, importing `AutoTokenizer` alone loads torch.\n",
